{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "tygerberg-sda-website",
  "main": "src/worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-01-09",
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // Without this, an unknown path returns a bare 404 with an empty body —
    // the default is "none", and a 404.html in dist is ignored. GitHub Pages
    // picks 404.html up on its own, so only Cloudflare needs telling.
    "not_found_handling": "404-page",
    // Which paths reach the Worker script at all.
    //
    // `/api/*` MUST be listed. The array form is not "also run the Worker for
    // these" — it is the whole set. Anything outside it is answered by asset
    // serving alone, and with `not_found_handling` above that means a path with
    // no file behind it gets 404.html instead of the Worker. Omitting `/api/*`
    // took out /api/live-status and /api/contact in production, and it took a
    // user report to find, because nothing here 404s at build time. C-60
    //
    // The headshot masters are the other path where the Worker has to see the
    // request. By default a URL that matches a file in `directory` is served
    // straight from the asset store and the Worker is never invoked — so the
    // guard in src/worker/index.ts, which rewrites a direct hit to the 1400px
    // copy, would never have run. C-51
    //
    // The negative pattern keeps the fallbacks on the default asset path: they
    // are what a browser is *meant* to get, and every Worker invocation here is
    // a billable request for an image that needs no logic.
    //
    // Add a route to src/worker/index.ts and you must add its path here too.
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/images/hod/*", "!/images/hod/fallback/*"]
  },
  // Workers Logs and Traces. The nested `logs` / `traces` blocks are the
  // per-signal switches and are what actually decides each one; the top-level
  // `enabled` is the older single toggle that only ever governed logs.
  //
  // `persist: true` keeps both in the Cloudflare dashboard, which is the only
  // place they are read from here — there is no external OTel destination
  // configured, so turning `persist` off would discard them entirely.
  //
  // `head_sampling_rate: 1` logs every request. Right for a site at this
  // traffic level; lower it if volume ever becomes a cost.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": false,
    "head_sampling_rate": 1,
    "logs": {
      "enabled": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 1,
      "persist": true,
      "invocation_logs": true
    },
    "traces": {
      "enabled": true,
      "persist": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 1
    }
  },
  "vars": {
    // When the LIVE badge is allowed to cost YouTube API quota, as a 5-field
    // cron expression read as a *window* (minute hour day-of-month month
    // day-of-week). Saturdays 09:00-12:59 SAST. Note cron day-of-week 6 is
    // Saturday (0 = Sunday); writing 7 would mean Sunday. Outside the window
    // no API call is made at all.
    //
    // The church streams 09:00-12:30. The hour field cannot express :30, and
    // erring short would drop the badge during the last half hour of a
    // service; erring long only permits polling, and the badge still appears
    // solely when YouTube reports a live stream. See C-14.
    //
    // Keep the minute field `*`. It controls whether the window is OPEN, not
    // how often YouTube is polled — narrowing it to `*\/5` would make the
    // badge vanish for the four minutes between each mark. Poll rate lives on
    // the client, in useLiveStatus.ts (every 5 minutes).
    "LIVE_CHECK_CRON": "* 9-12 * * 6",
    "LIVE_CHECK_TZ": "Africa/Johannesburg",

    // Contact-form delivery. These were Worker secrets; they are vars now by
    // decision — see C-31. They are role addresses on the operator's own
    // domain, not personal or church-member data, and the cost of publishing
    // them is spam to three mailboxes rather than exposure of anyone's
    // information. The From appears in every message header regardless.
    //
    // Setting a Worker *secret* of the same name is the way back: remove the
    // line here and `wrangler secret put` it, and no code changes.
    //
    // CONTACT_EMAIL_TO must be a verified destination address on the account.
    // Change it and update `allowed_destination_addresses` below to match, or
    // the binding rejects it.
    //
    // There is deliberately no CONTACT_EMAIL_BCC. It used to archive a copy to
    // notifications@cloudkid.link, which Email Routing hands to an unrelated
    // Worker nobody reads — and on the free path every recipient is verified,
    // so that second recipient could fail the whole send. See C-31. To archive
    // again, re-add the var here AND the address in
    // `allowed_destination_addresses` below, then redeploy.
    "CONTACT_EMAIL_TO": "hello@cloudkid.link",
    "CONTACT_EMAIL_FROM": "mailer@cloudkid.link",
    // Hostnames siteverify must echo back for /api/contact tokens — C-21.
    "TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES": "sdatygerberg.co.za"
  },
  // Lets /api/contact email the church directly.
  //
  // Fenced to exactly the addresses above. This became possible only once they
  // stopped being secrets (ADR-0001): while the destination was secret, naming
  // it here would have published it, so the binding had to stay unrestricted.
  // Now that it is public anyway, the fence costs nothing and bounds what a bug
  // in the Worker could do — a mistake in `contactEmail.ts` cannot mail a
  // stranger, it fails instead.
  //
  // Keep these in step with the `CONTACT_EMAIL_*` vars above. They are a
  // separate list, so changing an address in one place and not the other is a
  // send that Cloudflare rejects. See C-31.
  "send_email": [
    {
      "name": "EMAIL",
      "allowed_destination_addresses": ["hello@cloudkid.link"],
      "allowed_sender_addresses": ["mailer@cloudkid.link"]
    }
  ],
  // Consent records for /api/contact submissions — CONCERNS.md C-34.
  //
  // A bucket that already existed in the account and had nothing writing to it:
  // the enquiry BCC goes to notifications@, which Email Routing hands to the
  // cloudkid-link-r2-explorer Worker, and that Worker is configured with
  // `targetBucket: "email-notifications"`. So consent was demonstrable only as
  // a raw .txt email dump in a different bucket. This binding is what makes the
  // Worker that *validates* consent also the one that records it.
  //
  // Remove the binding and submissions still work: `storeConsent` treats an
  // unbound namespace as "not configured" and the enquiry email keeps carrying
  // the consent line, exactly as before.
  //
  // Retention is NOT set here — R2 lifecycle rules are dashboard-only. See
  // SETUP-INSTRUCTIONS.md §3.6; personal data with no expiry is a POPIA problem
  // that this file cannot solve.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "CONSENT",
      "bucket_name": "consent-cloudkid-link"
    }
  ],
  "routes": [
    // Production custom domain (the sdatygerberg.co.za zone lives in the
    // Cloudflare account) — `wrangler deploy` provisions/attaches this
    // automatically. The apex is canonical; www -> apex is a zone Redirect
    // Rule, not a Worker route (run_worker_first is path-scoped, so a host
    // redirect cannot live in the Worker). See SETUP-INSTRUCTIONS §domains
    // and C-61.
    { "pattern": "sdatygerberg.co.za", "custom_domain": true }
  ],

  // Devtest — the integration preview, deployed from `dev` by
  // deploy-devtest-cloudflare.yml. A SECOND Worker
  // (`tygerberg-sda-website-devtest`) on the old production hostname,
  // replacing the sunset GitHub Pages preview (C-61). It inherits `main`,
  // `assets` (so `run_worker_first` routes /api/* here too) and
  // `observability`; only the non-inheritable keys below are redeclared.
  //
  // Deliberately no `send_email`, `r2_buckets` or CONTACT_EMAIL_* vars: a
  // preview must never email the church or write consent records to the
  // production bucket, so /api/contact answers `not-configured` here. The LIVE
  // badge never lights either — the devtest Worker carries no YOUTUBE_API_KEY,
  // so liveStatus stays not-live and the latest-video card falls back to the
  // keyless embed-page scrape, which needs no secret.
  "env": {
    "devtest": {
      "name": "tygerberg-sda-website-devtest",
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "tygerberg-sda.cloudkid.link", "custom_domain": true }],
      "vars": {
        "LIVE_CHECK_CRON": "* 9-12 * * 6",
        "LIVE_CHECK_TZ": "Africa/Johannesburg",
        // Hostnames siteverify must echo back for /api/contact tokens — C-21.
        "TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES": "tygerberg-sda.cloudkid.link"
      }
      // No send_email, r2_buckets or CONTACT_EMAIL_* here on purpose: a preview
      // sends no church email and writes no consent records, so /api/contact
      // answers `not-configured` (C-61). wrangler prints an advisory warning
      // that these top-level bindings are not inherited — expected and correct;
      // declaring them empty only makes the warning noisier, so they are simply
      // omitted.
    }
  }
}
